aps)\nThe shape position/orientation has been changing. Let me check the current orientation by looking at the ascii.\nActually, from the segmentation data, the shape is at rows 11-25, cols 14-28. Let me check the ascii to see its orientation.\nBut more importantly, I need to figure out the GOAL. Let me think about what would complete the level:\n- If the goal is \"both shapes same color\", then I need both to be white (or both orange, or both green).\n- Currently: shape=O, frame=W. Not the same.\n- If I click again: shape=W, frame=N. Not the same.\nSo \"both same color\" doesn't work with just clicking red blocks. Maybe I need to combine SPACE (rotation) with MOUSE (color swap).\nLet me try SPACE and see if the colors change with rotation.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# H1 | MOUSE on red blocks toggles between two color states for both shapes\n# State A: White shape, Green frame\n# State B: Orange shape, White frame\n# Need to figure out the goal - maybe SPACE rotation matters\n\n# Let me check the current shape orientation via ascii\nascii = current_frame.ascii\nlines = ascii.split('\\n')\nprint(\"Shape area (current orientation):\")\nfor i in range(10, 27):\n    row = lines[i][10:30]\n    if 'O' in row or 'R' in row:\n        print(f\"{i:3d}: {row}\")\n\n# Try SPACE to see if rotation affects colors\naction(['SPACE'])\nseg = current_frame.segmentation\nnodes = seg['nodes']\nprint(\"\\nAfter SPACE:\")\nfor n in nodes:\n    if n['pixels'] > 5 and n['color'] not in ['S']:\n        b = n['boundary']\n        rows = [p[0] for p in b]\n        cols = [p[1] for p in b]\n        print(f\"  id={n['id']}, {n['color']}: rows {min(rows)}-{max(rows)}, cols {min(cols)}-{max(cols)}, pixels={n['pixels']}\")\nprint(f\"  gameplay_changed={last_action_result.get('gameplay_changed')}\")\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "T